course: dictionary
lesson: 3956
title: Gentle words for hard news
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are softer than the plain truth. Furlough is one of them. When a worker is on furlough, they are told not to come to work for a while, and the job is kept but paused. Context shows this: «Furlough was government help in 2020, during the pandemic, paying part of the wages of people who couldn't work.» The words «couldn't work» and «paying part of the wages» tell you the job still existed.
explain: Compare furlough with being let go. Being let go means losing your job. Furlough means you may come back to it. The sentence «People argue about whether the furlough scheme worked well» shows that a scheme is a plan, and it was a plan to keep jobs going, not to end them. Ask: if furlough meant losing a job, would the scheme keep jobs? It would not, so furlough must mean paused.
explain: Words like this are called euphemisms: gentler words used for something hard or unpleasant. Furlough sounds kinder than «out of a job». When you read news or letters, notice the soft word, then ask what plain word it is hiding.
fact: Furlough is a noun (a furlough, furloughs) and a verb (furlough, furloughs, furloughed, furloughing).
fact: Furlough means a time when workers are told not to come to work for a while, often because there is not enough work, and they may get less pay or none.
fact: «Furloughed» means sent home from work for a period, often unpaid.
fact: Furlough can also mean leave from duty for a soldier, or a short permitted absence from prison.
fact: A euphemism is a gentler word used for something hard or unpleasant: «on furlough» sounds softer than «out of a job».
exercise: «People argue about whether the furlough scheme worked well.» Find and copy one word from this sentence that means a plan. | *scheme | argue | well | people
exercise: Being «let go» and being «on furlough» are different. What is the main difference? | Being on furlough means a paid holiday | They mean exactly the same thing | *Being on furlough means the job is kept but paused; being let go means losing the job | Being on furlough means losing the job for ever; being let go means a pause
exercise: A soldier is given furlough. What does it mean? | A promotion to a higher rank | A new uniform | *Leave from duty for a short time | Being sent to prison for a long time
test: Which form of furlough is the past tense verb? | furlough | furloughs | *furloughed | furloughing
test: What is a euphemism? | A very long word with many syllables | A word that is always rude | *A gentler word used for something hard or unpleasant | A word that only comes from another country
exam: Which clue in «Furlough was government help in 2020, during the pandemic, paying part of the wages of people who couldn't work» shows the job was not lost? | *paying part of the wages | 2020 | during the pandemic | government help
